  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively provide only broad precursors: temperature-dependent HEA restructuring and diffusion, surface-energy effects in nanoscale metals, chemical clustering or ordering, and preferential loss or reaction of selected alloying elements. R6 is the closest nanoscale precedent because it combines temperature-dependent molecular dynamics with surface-energy-driven shape reorientation, while R2 suggests selective depletion during high-temperature laser processing and R9 studies a compositionally related Co-Ni-Cu-containing HEA under elevated-temperature simulation. However, none describes vacuum heating of multicomponent HEA nanoparticles, Au/Cu surface segregation driven by their low surface energies, reshaping-enhanced diffusion, or the resulting evaporation and disappearance of small particles. Thus the literature points generally toward thermally activated, surface-mediated evolution but leaves the specific intervention and coupled segregation-evaporation mechanism as a substantial new step."
